<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treblinka extermination camp</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1942">1942</a>–<a href="/years/1943">1943</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Treblinka</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Treblinka was a Nazi extermination camp north-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a> in German-occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, built as part of Operation Reinhard. Between July 1942 and October 1943 some 700,000 to 900,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, most of them from the Warsaw Ghetto, were murdered there in gas chambers, more than at any camp except <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oswiecim">Auschwitz</a>. Prisoners staged a revolt in August 1943, and the Germans demolished the camp soon after.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treblinka-extermination-camp.jpg" alt="Treblinka extermination camp" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Little Savage · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Treblinka_-_Rail_tracks.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Treblinka lay in a forest about 80 kilometres northeast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a>. Before the war the site held a gravel mining enterprise owned by the Polish industrialist Marian Łopuszyński, linked by rail to the main cities of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. The Germans found it well connected yet isolated, halfway between some of the largest ghettos in occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, among them the Warsaw Ghetto and the Białystok Ghetto. It consisted of two camps. Treblinka I, opened on 1 September 1941, was a forced-labour camp through which 20,000 people passed, and about half of them died of exhaustion, hunger and disease. Treblinka II, the extermination camp, was built from 10 April 1942.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Treblinka II was part of Operation Reinhard, the programme for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocide</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> outlined after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wannsee-conference">Wannsee Conference</a> of 20 January 1942. It had one function, to murder those sent there, and it was disguised as a transit camp with a false station, a painted clock and a ticket window. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deportation">Mass deportation</a> from the Warsaw Ghetto began on 22 July 1942, and the gas chambers began operating the next morning. German records give 265,000 Jews carried from the ghetto between 22 July and 12 September 1942. Almost 20,000 foreign Jews arrived between October 1942 and March 1943. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romani-people">Romani people</a> were also killed there.</p><p>Irmfried Eberl, the first commandant, was replaced by Franz Stangl on 1 September 1942, after the early gassing machinery broke down and the mass graves overflowed. From early 1943 the bodies were dug up and burned on open pyres. A small group of prisoners was kept alive to work, and in early 1943 they formed a resistance organisation. On 2 August 1943, about 700 prisoners revolted, set buildings ablaze and attacked the gate; most were shot, but about 200 escaped, half of whom were killed in pursuit. Around 70 are known to have survived the war, among them Samuel Willenberg. Gassing ended in October 1943 and the camp was dismantled, with a farmhouse built on the site and the ground ploughed over.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Soviet forces reached the site in August 1944. Treblinka was long looted by local people digging for valuables, and the site was not at first protected. A memorial designed by Franciszek Duszeńko was begun in 1958, and Treblinka was declared a national monument of martyrdom on 10 May 1964. The first German trials of former SS personnel were held at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dusseldorf">Düsseldorf</a> in 1964 and 1965. An exhibition centre opened in 2006. Estimates of the number of dead differ between scholars and museums.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Treblinka extermination camp” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In occupied Europe, mobile killing units of the Einsatzgruppen shot Jews in the Soviet territories from 1941, and the Wannsee Conference of January 1942 coordinated the deportation of Jews from across the continent to extermination camps in occupied Poland: Chełmno, Bełżec, Sobibór, <b>Treblinka</b>, Majdanek and Auschwitz-Birkenau.</blockquote><a href="/empire/nazi-germany">Nazi Germany · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Jewish fighters resisted German troops sent to deport the ghetto's remaining inhabitants to <b>Treblinka</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-19">19 April in history</a></li></ul></section>
